  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://ontology.local/support#Ticket">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ticket</rdfs:label>
    <skos:prefLabel>Ticket</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition>A single recorded request for support from a customer account, tracked from submission to resolution. One ticket represents one customer problem. It is owned by exactly one account, assigned to at most one agent at a time, carries exactly one severity, is governed by exactly one SLA, and may be escalated to development. It may be submitted by a Contact at the account or, where the customer buys through one, by a Partner acting on the account's behalf.</skos:definition>
    <rdfs:comment>A single recorded request for support from a customer account, tracked from submission to resolution. One ticket represents one customer problem. It is owned by exactly one account, assigned to at most one agent at a time, carries exactly one severity, is governed by exactly one SLA, and may be escalated to development. It may be submitted by a Contact at the account or, where the customer buys through one, by a Partner acting on the account's behalf.</rdfs:comment>
    <sup:domainType>Support</sup:domainType>
    <sup:status>Canonical</sup:status>
    <sup:identityRule>The CRM ticket/case number is authoritative. Ticket numbers are unique within a CRM but not across CRMs — a fully qualified ticket identity is `systemCode` + `ticketNumber`. This is a real collision risk in reporting.</sup:identityRule>
    <sup:sourceFile>Ontology/Entities/Ticket.md</sup:sourceFile>
    <skos:altLabel>Case</skos:altLabel>
